ly R, and Strada vastly outspends Pericola after a hard-fought primary win. The lone tied poll is old, partisan-sponsored, and too weak to override the structural advantage, though it argues for a wider uncertainty band.", "watch": "Any post-Labor Day independent polling showing Pericola within single digits, or a shift in the national environment toward Democrats, would tighten this race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 59 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Daniel Webster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Daniel Webster's retirement leaves this R+7 Central Florida seat open, and Republicans appear well positioned to hold it. Joe Strada emerged from a recount-verified primary win over Carey Baker with a formidable war chest of $2.2 million, while Democrat James Pericola has raised only about $120,000. Outside spending has favored Strada modestly.\n\nExpert raters uniformly place the race on the Republican side, with several calling it Solid or Safe R and markets pricing a Democratic win near 12 percent. A single July poll sponsored by a Democratic firm showed a 42-42 tie, but its age and sponsorship limit its reliability. The expectation is a comfortable but not automatic Republican victory.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1071, "margin": -7.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1312, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1199, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0703, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -9.0}]}